{
 "gdd": "design/gdd/player-controller-input.md",
 "blockers": [
  {
   "where": "AC-PCI-040 (L1117) and PC-18 test (10) (L232); sibling wording at L146, L202, L300, L1052 that the sync did rewrite",
   "issue": "The sync moved the Body's `previous := current` from `post_restore` to the kernel's `snap_baseline` on entry to RUNNING (ADR-0008 § 6.1), and L146/L202/L300/L1052 now say Body sets no `previous := current` in `post_restore`. AC-PCI-040 still says 'WHEN `post_restore` runs THEN `pos_prev == pos_curr == (302, 200)`', and PC-18 test (10) still says '`post_restore` yields `prev == curr`'. Both tests assert behaviour the rewritten rule forbids. An ADR-conformant Body fails them, and an implementer who makes them pass puts the baseline back into `post_restore`. The `sub`-preserved half is still correct.",
   "fix": "Reword both so the trigger is restore followed by the kernel's `snap_baseline` on entry to RUNNING, and the oracle is `pos_prev == pos_curr == (302, 200)` and `sub == (106, 0)`. Mark AC-PCI-040 as an integration check, since a Body-only unit test cannot call `snap_baseline`. Keep the assertion that Body's own `post_restore` does not touch `previous`. Update the `[PC-18 test 10; PC-17; pipeline C5]` tags to cite ADR-0008 § 6.1."
  },
  {
   "where": "PC-2 POINTER definition (L121), PC-5 mouse aim (L162), D.7 variable table row `cursor_vp, picture_centre, k_eff` (L496)",
   "issue": "The sync made the Adapter forward the cursor as raw root-viewport physical window px, 'unconverted', and moved the picture-origin subtraction to the Chart Table layer (`RenderLayout.picture_rect.position`). The aim path still defines `cursor_vp` as 'relative to the world picture's origin' and uses it with `picture_centre = (L_w, L_h)·k_z/2`, which is picture-relative. L162 and L496 also say the Adapter takes the value 'unconverted'. No text says who subtracts `picture_rect.position` for aim. The pipeline GDD (D.14b, L634) defines `c_pic` as picture-relative. An implementer who reads 'unconverted' feeds window px into `(cursor_vp − picture_centre)/k_eff`, so the beam aim is off by the letterbox offset whenever the window is not an exact integer fit. The old text removed the double-subtraction risk; the new text reintroduced a gap. This is the Pillar 1 chest-beam anchor.",
   "fix": "In L162 and L496, say that the Adapter reads the root viewport mouse position in physical window px with no inverse stretch transform, then subtracts `RenderLayout.picture_rect.position` to form the picture-relative `cursor_vp`. Alternatively, define `cursor_vp` and `picture_centre` in window px. Add the offset to the AC-PCI-056/059 fixtures with a non-zero letterbox so the AC can discriminate. State on L121 that the Controller's aim path and the Chart Table layer each subtract exactly once."
  }
 ]
}
